<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baiyue</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hundred Yue</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baiyue, or Hundred Yue, were the various non-Chinese peoples of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in the first millennium BCE, living in states such as Minyue, Nanyue and Ouyue. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> conquered much of their land in the late 3rd century BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> absorbed their kingdoms by 110 BCE, gradually assimilating them into Chinese culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baiyue.jpg" alt="Baiyue" width="320" height="611" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Discott · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yue_statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Baiyue, Hundred Yue, or simply Yue, were various ethnic groups of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, known for their short hair, body tattoos, fine swords and naval prowess. The term Baiyue first appears in the Lüshi Chunqiu, compiled around 239 BCE, and was later used as a collective term for many non-Huaxia, non-Han populations of the south. According to Chunming Wu, their prehistoric ancestors were active seafarers who communicated with proto-Austronesians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and the Pacific. The Baiyue were mostly Austronesian and Kra-Dai in ethnicity, though some were perhaps related to Hmong-Mien or Austroasiatic groups, and they were not necessarily related to one another.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 9th century BCE two Yue tribes, the Gouwu and the Yuyue, came under the cultural influence of their northern Chinese neighbours. Traditional accounts attribute the exchange to Taibo, a Zhou prince who had gone into exile in the south. In the Spring and Autumn period the Gouwu founded the state of Wu and the Yuyue the state of Yue. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, those states were absorbed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> armies advanced south along the Xiang River into modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>. He allegedly sent half a million troops in five armies against the Yue, who defeated the first attack and killed the Qin commander.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo">Zhao Tuo</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> general born around 240 BCE in the state of Zhao, served as magistrate of Nanhai. After the Qin fell in 206 BCE he had also conquered Guilin and Xiang, and he declared himself King Wu of Nanyue, with his capital at Panyu, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a>, he respected Yue customs, let local chieftains keep their traditions and encouraged settlers to intermarry with the Yue. In 111 BCE the Han conquered Nanyue and divided it into nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> and two outpost commands, seeking its maritime trade routes and luxury goods such as pearls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Periods of war in the north sent waves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> southward, and intermarriage with the indigenous peoples quickened sinicisation. By the Tang dynasty Yue had become largely a regional name. Features of southern Chinese vocabulary, phonology and syntax are attributed to Yue substrate languages, and Cantonese kept some features of the extinct Nanyue language. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> derives from Nam Viet, the Vietnamese form of Nanyue. Vietnamese tradition tells of Van Lang, a state of the Lac Viet founded c. 2879 BCE and ruled by the Hung kings, though the history of the Lac Viet begins only in the 7th century BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baiyue” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 214 BCE Qin Shi Huang kept roughly 100,000 men on the northern border and sent some 500,000 south against the <b>Baiyue</b> peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Wu Rui's support among the <b>Baiyue</b> peoples of the south gave him a following beyond the Chinese population.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hengshan">Kingdom of Hengshan</a></li><li><blockquote>The Min Yue were a <b>Baiyue</b> people of Fujian.</blockquote><a href="/state/minyue">Minyue</a></li><li><blockquote>Zhao Tuo was a Qin officer sent south in the conquest of the <b>Baiyue</b> lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/nanyue">Nanyue</a></li></ul></section>
